('P', '**Stress reactions are common.** In a national survey 3 to 5 days after the September 11, 2001 attacks, 44% of U.S. adults reported at least one substantial stress symptom, even far from the attacks, and 35% of parents said their children had stress symptoms (Schuster et al., 2001). Most acute reactions fade without becoming a disorder.'),
('P', '**A limited predictor of PTSD.** Across 22 studies, an ASD diagnosis predicted later PTSD reasonably well in those who had it, but most people who went on to develop PTSD had **not** met ASD criteria. The author concluded that describing the full range of early stress reactions is more useful than relying on ASD to predict PTSD (Bryant, 2011).'),
